; `(absent …)` over two premises.
;
; `(absent P)` is negation as failure over **one** premise. This rule reads
; as "no q and no p", and `(p A)` is stated below, so a reader expects it not
; to fire; read at one premise, it would drop the second in silence. The
; grammar refuses the second argument at the parse, where it starts
; (Q-M1e.18: `absent` is read only at its own arity).
;
; The fix for a program that means the conjunction is to write it: wrap the
; two in an `(and …)`, which is what `std.macro`'s `forall` expands to.
(relation p T)
(relation q T)

(rule two-subject-naf ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent (q ?x) (p ?x)))
  :assert (q ?x))

(p A)
